{"title":"GATE 2027-Subjectwise Test- 11 (Analog Electronics)","fileName":"040_GATE 2027-Subjectwise Test- 11 (Analog Electronics).html","branch":"ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING","series":"GATE 2027 Online Test Series - Electrical Engineering","provider":"MADE EASY","duration":5400,"questions":[{"qnum":1,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>The oscillator circuit shown in the figure has an ideal inverting amplifier. With <em>R </em>= 10 k&Omega; and <em>C </em>= 10 nF, its frequency of oscillation (in Hz) is</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/c063ee5a567957d37f3f8fcda43a0b24ec3d7d5d.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p><em>f</em> = 650 Hz</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p><em>f </em>= 1591 Hz</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p><em>f </em>= 265 Hz</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p><em>f</em> = 325 Hz</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/9ff5b0172f005d0e4c1c3411fccb11b4fc6ff1a4.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +1 / -0.33"},{"qnum":2,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>An amplifier circuit is shown in the given figure. Assume standard parameters for silicon and given component V<sub>CC</sub> = 12 V, R<sub>C</sub> = 4 k&Omega;, R<sub>b1</sub> = 60 k&Omega;, R<sub>b2</sub> = 30 k&Omega;, R<sub>e</sub> = 3.3 k&Omega; and &beta; = 150. Use standard values like V<sub>BE(on)</sub> = 0.7 V and V<sub>T</sub> = 25 mV. The magnitude of the voltage gain |A<sub>V|</sub> = |V<sub>0</sub>/V<sub>s</sub>|.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/e3f7a3ff668b17dbfefd6280b46c7c933d6ba0f7.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>4</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>153</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>100</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>160</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/df1080ba2fac9da6afc8af7f741c015199fbb208.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/b499de1167269c6de639a8ba5b20e198f1286d09.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/9a9cc3305dec26afe2601ac1ccbe454df5bd6157.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +2 / -0.66"},{"qnum":3,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>For the NPN silicon transistor circuit shown with the new specified components, the collector current (I<sub>C</sub>) is</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/238a1e46088f1c9fe9d98c0be6dc651d67863564.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>0 mA</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>2.2 mA</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>2.87 mA</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>10.0 mA</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/982616512586e7e180dc545ce49450bf4e37d06b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +1 / -0.33"},{"qnum":4,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>For the figure shown below, the values of V<sub>0</sub> and I<sub>D</sub> are respectively: (cut-in voltage of diode = 0.7 V)</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/3c0e34e0ca1ba4a74ff24709785b764e48f0351d.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>11 V and 1.1 mA</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>22 V and 2.2 mA</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>33 V and 1.1 mA</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>22 V and 1.1 mA</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>Since the direction of the resultant current is the same as that of arrows of both diodes so, both the diodes will be forward biased. Replacing the diodes by their voltages and series resistance models, we get as</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/5e99055e482c926c93a48c335dc96ba210f52bd5.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/c06f6e96cc5daca877b32dd6fbfa34fc17b42a3d.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +1 / -0.33"},{"qnum":5,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>For the network shown in figure, which of the following represents the range of values for <em>V<sub>i </sub></em>that will maintain <em>V<sub>L</sub> </em>at 8 V and not exceed the maximum power rating of the zener diode?</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/e221d6fe0d2f163dadeae921f2b8ff6c3835a514.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>11.3 V to 15.86 V</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>8 V to 15.86 V</p></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/ebc025d676b9374e89a96a58986736090c9aae10.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/4405f1a7c0fb2d6299c1fefe5f033b1709f78fbc.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +1 / -0.33"},{"qnum":6,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>The inverting OP-AMP circuit has an open-loop gain A<sub>OL</sub> = 100. Based on the given schematic (R<sub>1</sub> = 1 k&Omega;, R<sub>2</sub> = 10 k&Omega;), determine the actual closed-loop gain <img src=\"/media/171bfc7e6b6911907e9791a96471fc8f425fa867.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /> accounting for the finite open-loop gain.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/636eb408cea908dae2ef7913f05b620e5b5bd5cd.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>&ndash;8.9</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>&ndash;9.0</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>&ndash;10.0</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>&ndash;11.2</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/a07858cf4a382f6dd30994bde03666d2dc865c89.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/563e564404ae1739378ab2457a3877d1e887b364.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +2 / -0.66"},{"qnum":7,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>For the inverting amplifier circuit with a T-network feedback configuration shown below, the voltage gain V<sub>out</sub>/V<sub>in</sub>, assuming the operational amplifier is ideal.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/b7c4c8e4b4becb03904a52594882997e22180502.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>-20</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>-120</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>-12</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>-200</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/c92cea2ee2cd83a7ca4b7403f5918ee8db297fea.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/5ecc0854c2bb625b8ba7ab17ca967ca76d33b26f.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +1 / -0.33"},{"qnum":8,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>The output voltage V<sub>0</sub> for the operational amplifier shown in circuit is, (assuming the op-amp is ideal)</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/9a88b633adaaaebca114207db65396003b08bf38.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>1.5 V</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>&ndash;1.5 V</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>&ndash;2.5 V</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>2.5 V</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/1514a8edcf6bebb1ebdb751b5a61e2c106e51d8c.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/395cd54e418db8484c728f925421b16d2dba4a70.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/b71031595236c7dfb30581e952bf5c3341e77ce4.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +2 / -0.66"},{"qnum":9,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>In the transformer coupled amplifier stage shown V<sub>BE</sub> = 0.7 V, &beta; = 50, and the quiescent voltage is V<sub>CB</sub> = 4 V. Then value of Re is</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/212cd2a85daa5c3c2cf2f166a49669577de99a91.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>2.57 k&Omega;</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>10 k&Omega;</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>5 k&Omega;</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>2.04 &Omega;</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>First applying KVL in B-E loop, the voltage drop across R<sub>e</sub> will be 6 &ndash; 0.7 = 5.3 V.<br />Now, applying KVL in C-E loop<br />12 + 6 = 4.3I<sub>C</sub> + 4 + 5.3<br />&there4; I<sub>C</sub> = 2.02 mA</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/6c3b1088bbc813de8d69c34efd02ab1c8dadc1f7.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +2 / -0.66"},{"qnum":10,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>For the ideal op-amp circuit with resistor values (R<sub>f</sub> = 120 k&Omega;, R<sub>in1</sub> = R<sub>in2</sub> = 2 k&Omega;, R<sub>g</sub> = 150 k&Omega;) shown above, the Common-Mode Rejection Ratio (CMRR).</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/1345431c48b67af29947a4d640c9898bf4612d04.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>155</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>205</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>305</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>455</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/d0d7f300a5c2ab4e3821a100cf22c07a37f59c1f.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/683fdfa72aea9679db1cbc8d30dd3583d433faf4.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +2 / -0.66"},{"qnum":11,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>For the NPN silicon transistor amplifier circuit shown, determine the actual magnitude of the voltage gain |A<sub>v</sub>| = |V<sub>0</sub>/V<sub>s</sub>|, accounting for realistic bias point values. Assume standard<br />parameters: V<sub>BE(on)</sub> = 0.7 V, V<sub>T</sub> = 25 mV, and &beta; = 150.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/0368a86399784d6df48c1d0d103b88e555b6a354.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>|A<sub>v</sub>| = 97.4</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>|A<sub>v</sub>| = 142.1</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>|A<sub>v</sub>| = 161.09</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>|A<sub>v</sub>| = 194.8</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/db2317cb6baad112e5f8799b0f301c28ac4f20cc.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/f12e893077957f416910e284961c6456383d593d.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/a8606b8403f7dc580dc534dd9f27193983aa7649.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/5143a9fba0145f358d8fe80751a25a6dbcbe4219.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +2 / -0.66"},{"qnum":12,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Consider the full wave rectifier circuit shown below:</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/ac636a118a06849d31d18c8873610ef3da549f3f.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>Assume the diodes are ideal and input voltage as shown below:</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/b107de1186fff2b43e6de2d1caa76c4e0bc418bf.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>The output waveform across load is represented as</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p><img src=\"/media/b166674649d3b2f9af7e7a897fd1be7a451183da.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p><img src=\"/media/f1cf4a353f2b991d5f9dd77cc6c91e341567b8a7.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p><img src=\"/media/7bf98758ecb46a6010bb22a87b47b052abeeacd9.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p><img src=\"/media/febd4df711e943764fb8f54eb2064227bf6bbb19.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/208ba472706310b7cd96047c7f8a3e1400a3d7f3.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/860bcc7e133a00acb7c7a38da3f74234d39090eb.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +1 / -0.33"},{"qnum":13,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Consider the below circuit with ideal op-amp and diodes.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/6e6545a2dbb757179b8eabf585be083208552fc1.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>Assume V<sub>in</sub>(t) = 2&pi;sin(2000&pi;t) Volts.<br />Average value of the output voltage<img src=\"/media/2f180884b0e55d04e3b5d7e79a2a3b8f30e9f71d.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /> is</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>1 Volt</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>&pi; Volt</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>&ndash;1 Volt</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>0 Volt</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/51794448daa0da4d3d6bb9b052f040eb7954d822.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +1 / -0.33"},{"qnum":14,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Consider the below circuit:</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/9ecce05cae6b71499324616bd57b375034cd42cc.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>Assume the diodes are ideal.<br />State of diode D<sub>1</sub>, D<sub>2</sub> and D<sub>3</sub> is:</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>D<sub>1</sub> ON, D<sub>2</sub> OFF, D<sub>3</sub> ON</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>D<sub>1</sub> ON, D<sub>2</sub> OFF, D<sub>3</sub> OFF</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>D<sub>1</sub> OFF, D<sub>2</sub> OFF, D<sub>3</sub> OFF</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>D<sub>1</sub> OFF, D<sub>2</sub> ON, D<sub>3</sub> ON</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/80cb521c7d0cc23b4e1bb08a7966879a03394ec2.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +1 / -0.33"},{"qnum":15,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Consider the given circuit, in which op-amp has a bias current of 2 mA as indicated in figure and otherwise op-amp is ideal. Zener knee current I<sub>Z min</sub> = 1 mA and Zener breakdown voltage V<sub>z</sub> = 4.7 volt.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/fd1e26f660da26eeaeea6abcf04db215fa21eac8.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>Load current I<sub>L</sub> as indicated in circuit is</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>136.36 &mu;A</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>2.09 mA</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>90.9 &mu;A</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>136.36 mA</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/aec0aceffd6b3b47759024694056f5b3dbd34787.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +1 / -0.33"},{"qnum":16,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Given: V<sub>1</sub> &ndash; V<sub>2</sub> = V<sub>in</sub>; R<sub>2</sub> = R<sub>1</sub> = 10 k&Omega;, + V<sub>sat</sub> = +10 V, &ndash;V<sub>sat</sub> = &ndash;10 V</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/ee3928a42e6e870a626b74b4a20219315467d8a4.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>Which one of the following represents the correct transfer characteristics i.e., V<sub>0</sub> versus V<sub>in</sub> plot for the above circuit?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p><img src=\"/media/cf1bb4e5bd2b6242bcfdbaad3648437fce3f1cfe.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p><img src=\"/media/5d03d2fbed579ec23b6e3fde9d9b0b25f15c1f6e.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p><img src=\"/media/5f04e77a7b269166ac79e9df14639694d63e5092.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p><img src=\"/media/01602da661c661e89ee0f19343696b6b29fd7132.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/9b7461bca4502fa3bea421b5f6e87d79c271e288.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/96daf2daf8fb4fc388d7792c91ba2eb54b31feb5.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/428a3df73c9a422d1f9fe539d7b0d9e1d6565a2a.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +2 / -0.66"},{"qnum":17,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Consider the zener voltage regulator circuit shown in figure.<br />Under the following operating conditions:<br />20 V &le; V<sub>S</sub> &le; 30 V, 1 &Omega; &le; R<sub>L</sub> &le; 10 &Omega;, I<sub>Z</sub> min = 10 mA,<br />P<sub>Z</sub> = 50 W and V<sub>Z</sub> = 10 V<br />where P<sub>Z</sub> &rarr; Power rating of Zener diode<br />V<sub>Z</sub> &rarr; Zener breakdown voltage<br />The range of resistor R<sub>s</sub> to keep the Zener diode in it&rsquo;s regulation range is given by</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/a05e98245b9dd7ec4eb2113209e1e36b8da48f9c.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>0.999 &Omega; &le; R<sub>S</sub> &le; 3.333 &Omega;</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>1.333 &Omega; &le; R<sub>S</sub> &le; 9.900 &Omega;</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>R<sub>S</sub> &le; 3.333 &Omega;</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>No range of R<sub>S</sub> is possible for given specification</p></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/e685530c3ed46a4d493c3a89e89e14a5dbab2fcd.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/9a6837b201f5b3def481babff45b069a2e0eb617.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>Thus to meet given specification no range (or) value of R<sub>s</sub> is possible.</p>","subject":"single | Marks +2 / -0.66"},{"qnum":18,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>The diode current I<sub>D</sub> in the circuit shown below when the diode has cut in voltage, V<sub>g</sub> = 0.7 V and forward resistance, R<sub>f</sub> = 25 &Omega; is ______ A.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/49876e2b0b0aa0032ea0750340e1f58008ba8f15.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>\n<div><span>Numerical answer type</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"15.5","high":"15.8"},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/e69f7845dc8610b481a1ce70da9f6db647a41d2d.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/27ac23d95eccc3c1f025468540550c908829cf74.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"numerical | Marks +1"},{"qnum":19,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>In the level shifter circuit shown in the figure, the value of R for V<sub>0</sub> to become zero. Assume V<sub>D</sub> = V<sub>BE</sub> = 0.7 V and h<sub>FE</sub> is very large is ______ &Omega;.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/291ce69ffbd187702b0e668d1846be8d18397c06.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>\n<div><span>Numerical answer type</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"2.95","high":"3.15"},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/2e207f6532184e34f67e97648f85f8f1ad62d693.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/5a0770273dd2b9f58dd3ef69b0be14203dc1880a.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"numerical | Marks +2"},{"qnum":20,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,B,C,D","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>For the circuit shown, neglecting base currents.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/aab1e8f535b61c36b0a9a81a8055c76833950c63.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>Which of the following is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>I<sub>0</sub> = 1 mA</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>I<sub>1</sub> = 4.67 mA</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>I<sub>2 </sub>= 3.5 mA</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>I<sub>3</sub> = 2.8 mA</p></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/96853e21f0cbae550c85b22ffa784b8ac24887b6.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"multi | Marks +2"},{"qnum":21,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>The output &ndash;V<sub>0</sub> of the circuit shown in figure. The value of R for which the output of the circuit becomes 0 V is _______ &Omega;.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/e90e705ad118180baf29b3e8450535e552ade82c.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>\n<div><span>Numerical answer type</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"2.8","high":"2.9"},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/4b42cd3ef0a7d91d215e88f26022c8d78660e4d2.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/c7aae18431a6328c7f358688972587f74bf37fa7.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/ea8b8f7f393c511a522a12c669423c54c365b22c.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"numerical | Marks +2"},{"qnum":22,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>The circuit shown in figure has ideal opamp, the circuit act as</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>lowpass filter</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>high pass filter</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>band pass filter</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>band stop filter</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/5a5d64c502276c48371692135889bffa15846468.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/8d768c50c038e4e9e131f049f06d03f0b9676fd7.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +2 / -0.66"},{"qnum":23,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>The circuit shown in the figure is an example of</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/eccb5f82b23cf1e4950903874c57d3b1bf151b93.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>series - shunt feedback</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>shunt - series feedback</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>shunt - shunt feedback</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>series - series feedback</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>We can see that V<sub>0</sub> is voltage across R<sub>L</sub> and feedback element is R<sub>2</sub>. Since feedback element is directly connected to V<sub>0</sub> [voltage across R<sub>L</sub>]. So we sample voltage. Also the feedback element is connected in shunt with signal source at input so we are mixing current. Thus it is shunt - shunt feedback.</p>","subject":"single | Marks +1 / -0.33"},{"qnum":24,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>The circuit was designed using ideal diodes and resistors as shown below</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/d2d5c588db228b77451534b9f78abd92c5b81160.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>The plot of V<sub>0</sub> with respect to time is</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p><img src=\"/media/210391413963d1cc6aa35af0552a23da913d2fd8.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p><img src=\"/media/d72c23a7ba3c435dc547f7543f41c5355ab25fa8.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p><img src=\"/media/32748cc643d8e4e7701b4fc9947e36d15ac8cea7.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>None of the above</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/3b2c0a89fd7bf1fd7ff6178a661917a7e83e5a17.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/9cdf887cc4fff832393daed8565ed6f7008c70ba.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/fad77cae56ba16041a2f439d66795fc9b4411e8b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +2 / -0.66"},{"qnum":25,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>The voltage regulation was designed using zener diode with V<sub>Z </sub>= 10 V at I<sub>Z</sub> = 2 mA and r<sub>Z</sub> = 0 &Omega;. The P<sub>max </sub>for Zener diode is 50 mW, then the range of values for resistor R<sub>S</sub> can be</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/2485221e98fa32c05f2490d0b37c4522ddf17d8b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>R<sub>s</sub> &ge; 1.66 k&Omega;</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>R<sub>s</sub> &le; 1.66 k&Omega;</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>2 k&Omega; &le; R<sub>s</sub> &le; 15 k&Omega;</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>1.66 k&Omega; &le; R<sub>s </sub>&le; 10 k&Omega;</p></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/2cf68f2cd1f03cec125d0e0a6e2f82dfb9c7dab6.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/9738bd08a54d140abca37e6448a75b9e6151ec83.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +2 / -0.66"},{"qnum":26,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>The trans conductance of device is g<sub>m</sub> = 1 mS then the value of input resistance will be _______ &Omega;.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/1da3df07ec12f6147e5dbef3694336b3a3152b16.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>\n<div><span>Numerical answer type</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"830","high":"835"},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/8f05d5f26ec2dc833217c8d871bfa0fc4e7bbffd.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/a21498c60f842c620f505b56bb616d8f07139040.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/acf8bc9cb80130ad8265e18ea2cddff801143be2.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"numerical | Marks +2"},{"qnum":27,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>The figure shown below represents a schmitt trigger</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/2c904eb0478fd6823dfd5616414782f3eb5e143a.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>Then the width of the hysteresis loop will be</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>12 V</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>8 V</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>13 V</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>10 V</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/093a81ee4ffe2df7cff1971bf726cadf2dc33617.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +1 / -0.33"},{"qnum":28,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>In the circuit shown below, the voltage across the 900 &Omega; resistor will be ________ V.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/f19796c645d42bf8a0ca9d2200e2f9103f33e75b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>(Given that breakdown voltage of zener diodes z<sub>1 </sub>and z<sub>2</sub> are 30 V and 10 V respectively).</p></div>\n<div><span>Numerical answer type</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"9","high":"11"},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/e2db1ef244a0cec3d3c1cf8996d53cab157b1ea2.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/d76b510fa547a82f8deb43e0a6abed2cb3f756e1.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"numerical | Marks +1"},{"qnum":29,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>In the figure shown below, all the op-amps are ideal.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/a559600088f33e774e353a73941d15b1241bd7de.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>The output voltage V<sub>0</sub> is ________ V.</p></div>\n<div><span>Numerical answer type</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"-17","high":"-17"},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/b1949c466c262f5904e911ac3392e00d9ec55ca9.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"numerical | Marks +1"},{"qnum":30,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Input and output impedances of the amplifier shown below are respectively,</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/b1abc31c2b7ff834862cf271de23a299237f355b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>0.833 k&Omega;, 120 k&Omega;</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>110 k&Omega;, 120 k&Omega;</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":